[Music]  people clutching their last few dollars  might find themselves on the brink of  despair  that puts them at risk of slipping into  psychological poverty traps  vicious cycles of financial distress and  mental illness building on one another  making poverty and disorders like  depression harder and harder to escape  freeing people from these traps requires  inexpensive solutions  on a large scale hoping to inform these  solutions  a new review in science highlighted  studies that examine psychological  poverty traps  with a few exceptions much of the  research took place in developing  countries  when changes in policy or climate  affected people's financial standing and  mental health  one study in indonesia compared  depression and suicide rates  among different villages receiving cash  transfers from a government program  a model based on census data estimated  1065 fewer suicides  occurred in the villages that were first  to get cash  the same study tracked the village's  rainfall totals which can affect farmers  incomes  villages that got lots of rain had less  depression and suicide than  drought-stricken areas  another program in oregon chose a group  of low-income adults  by lottery to receive free health  insurance worth 500 to 750  after a few months insurance lottery  winners  were 25 percent less likely to have  depression  in addition to studies linking income  changes to depression  the review noted the other side of the  coin  low-cost depression treatments that  improved economic outcomes  a randomized trial in goa india tested  the effects of cognitive behavioral  therapy on depressed adults  many of them unemployed at a cost of 66  dollars per person  lay counselors encouraged people to  focus on activities they enjoyed  say gardening or music to ease their  negative thoughts and attitudes  a control group of other adults receive  more traditional medical attention  such as primary care visits after 12  months  adults in the experimental group were 60  percent more likely to have recovered  and they worked on average 2.3 more days  a month  giving them the chance to make more  money in pakistan  pregnant women volunteered for a similar  program that offer cognitive behavioral  therapy from lay counselors  a control group simply received visits  from primary care doctors  who advise them on issues like  immunizations and breastfeeding  after just six months women receiving  therapy were less than half as likely to  be depressed as the control group  seven years later they had also gained  more say  in their family spending decisions their  children even ended up attending better  schools  suggesting the therapy's benefits might  span generations  programs like the ones in india and  pakistan have since been adapted to  treat maternal depression  in the united states and canada  the reviews findings indicate that  policy has a real effect  on breaking psychological poverty traps  a reminder that it's possible  to intervene and reduce mental and  financial hardship  you

